## New skills, approaches, etc. I used and where I grew the most during this project

- **How to merge dataframes:** I had never attempted to merge dataframes before, and it required me to analyze my data and use Pandas meticulously to decide what to merge and how to do it properly.

- **Handling big/messy datasets:** This was the biggest dataset I've used thus far, and there were many things that forced me to rethink how I would code it and analyze my data, as well as push me to narrow my focus for my story. Overall, this made me much more attentive to what I was recording and using in my report.

- **Learned how to pinpoint issues/vague areas in datasets:** Besides duplicates, there were some unclear parts of the data I discovered while coding, like finding areas with the most frequent shutdowns versus areas with the longest shutdowns, given that some shutdowns were considered one event but recorded separately due to changes in the shutdown.

- **Illustrator and scrollytelling:** I had wanted to apply my visual skills more in previous projects but did not have the time. I was able to use both scrollytelling and Illustrator for this project.

- **Using a second dataset:** In my previous projects I had wanted to include an additional dataset but struggled to find one during the timeframe I had. For this project, I found two (I originally had one for NSDP that is still in my notebook) and chose to use the World Bank CSV mentioned above.

---

## What I tried to do but did not have the skill or time to do

I didn't have time to find many other datasets to incorporate besides the World Bank dataset that I included in my final draft of my report. I wanted to include TRAI Telecom Subscription Data reports, but learned they were divided into monthly PDFs, which became too time-consuming by the time this project was due. If I had more time, I would like to have used this dataset.